   146	`0≤A_i≤4`, `S_i=14−3A_i`, `T_i=3A_i+1−C`, `C=2ΣA_i−13`.
   147	
   148	In particular, `7≤A≤12`. For fixed A_i, all nonnegative off-diagonal integer matrices B can be specified by `x=B_01`. Set `K=T_1−S_2+T_0`. Then
   149	
   150	`max(0,K−S_1,K−T_0) ≤ x ≤ min(S_0,K,S_2+K−T_0)`.
   151	
   152	The other five entries follow from the row and column sums. Exact enumeration gives respectively **18,12,7,6,3,1** feasible ordered A_i signatures for A=7,8,9,10,11,12. These are only aggregate signatures, not constructed blocks.
   153	
   154	### 4.2 The exact missing global object
   155	
   156	A construction in this action requires:
   157	
   158	1. 27 pairwise disjoint fixed KTSs, each specified by 14 fixed transversal class-orbits and one 29-class orbit with the seed signature above.
   159	2. Two ordinary resolved KTS seeds, each containing at most one triple from any C29 orbit, whose 29 translates are pairwise disjoint.
   160	3. Across these fixed and free constituents, every one of the 3,655 triple-orbits assigned exactly once: a fixed constituent consumes full orbits, while a free seed consumes one representative per assigned orbit.
   161	
   162	The fixed constituents consume `27·43=1,161` triple-orbits. The two free seeds must consume the remaining `2·1,247=2,494`. In particular **all 42 forbidden-AAA AP orbits must occur in the two free seeds**, never in the fixed constituents. No such coupled package is constructed here; eliminating the 56-fixed pattern does not solve the surviving 27-fixed case.
   163	
   164	## 5. A genuinely noncyclic regular route at 57
   165	
   166	Changing from `Z_55` to CRT coordinates `Z_5×Z_11` changes nothing. A genuinely different regular group of order 55 is
   167	
   168	`G=C_11 ⋊ C_5`, `(x,y)(x',y')=(x+3^y x' mod11, y+y' mod5)`.
   169	
   170	Here `3^5=1 mod11` and 3 has order 5. The group is nonabelian. Let it act by left multiplication on its 55 points and fix u,w. All triple orbits have length 55, because their stabilizers have order dividing both 55 and 6. The infinity-pair completion map bijects the 55 constituents of any G-invariant LKTS with G, so its constituent action is necessarily regular. Equivalently, this route requires one resolved KTS transverse to the G triple-orbits.
   171	
   172	This is not the scalar model eliminated in §1: G is the **development group**, not a group fixing one base block set. At 87 there is no analogous nonabelian group of order 85: Sylow counting makes both the order-5 and order-17 subgroups normal, hence the group is cyclic. Smaller development groups or other point-orbit decompositions are not excluded.
   173	
   174	### 5.1 Exact two-infinity starter package
   175	
   176	Normalize the base infinity-pair block to `{u,w,e}`. For a finite edge `{a,b}`, its left-translation pair orbit is the inverse class `[a^(-1)b]={a^(-1)b,b^(-1)a}`. There are 27 such nonidentity classes.
   177	
   178	A sufficient and necessary auxiliary package for the regular G route is:
   179	
   180	- Two edge-disjoint perfect matchings `M_u,M_w` on `G\{e}`, each using every nonidentity inverse-difference class exactly once.
   181	- A bijection π from M_u to M_w pairing only edges with disjoint endpoints.
   182	- 477 finite triangles, covering exactly once every edge of `K_G − M_u − M_w`, and representing exactly once every G-orbit of finite triples.
   183	- A partition of those triangles into one class of 18 triangles partitioning `G\{e}`, and 27 classes of 17 triangles: for each `E∈M_u`, its triangles partition `G\(E∪π(E))`.
   184	
   185	Attach `{u,w,e}` to the first class and `{u}∪E`, `{w}∪π(E)` to the other classes. The pair conditions give an STS, the point partitions give its resolution, and orbit transversality gives the full LKTS by G-development. Conversely every such base KTS yields this package.
   186	
   187	The π condition itself is not an additional obstruction once the two starters are edge-disjoint: an edge of one matching meets exactly two edges of the other, so the allowed bipartite graph is 25-regular and has a perfect matching. The difficult still-missing object is the **simultaneous transverse triangle decomposition and its specified point-disjoint class complements**, not just a pair of starters. No Frobenius construction is claimed, and the extended solver allocation was instead directed to the surviving reflection model of §3.
