     1	# Large sets of Kirkman triple systems: orders 57 and 87
     2	
     3	## Source
     4	
     5	- Book: *10000 Selected Problems in Sciences: Mathematics* (Science Press, 2009).
     6	- Problem: **柯克曼三元系大集的存在性问题 / Existence Problem of Large Sets of Kirkman Triple Systems**.
     7	- Author: **常彦勋 (Yanxun Chang)**, Beijing Jiaotong University.
     8	- Book pages **428–429**, PDF pages **444–445**. Exact extracted text: [`sources/book-excerpt.txt`](sources/book-excerpt.txt).
     9	- The book asks for all orders `v=6t+3`, **t≥1**, and says 21 was its smallest unsettled order. The degenerate order 3 also satisfies the definition below and is admitted by the checker, but is not part of the book's t≥1 quantifier.
    10	
    11	## Problem
    12	
    13	On the labelled point set `X={0,…,v−1}`, an STS(v) is a set of unordered 3-element subsets in which every unordered pair of points occurs exactly once. A parallel class partitions X into triples. A KTS(v) is an STS(v) together with a partition of its blocks into `(v−1)/2` parallel classes. An LKTS(v) consists of **v−2** pairwise block-disjoint KTS(v)s; equivalently, their blocks partition all `binom(v,3)` triples. Resolutions may differ independently between constituents; no compatibility beyond disjoint blocks is required.
    14	
    15	The **book's full question** is existence for every `v≡3 (mod 6)`, v≥9. The **two finite targets of this investigation** are LKTS(57) and LKTS(87). Search restrictions (cyclic development and, in some runs, a reflection) are not part of the problem.
    16	
    17	## Status as of 2026-10-14
    18	
    19	### (a) Verbatim book question and faithful translation
    20	
    21	> 对于任意 v = 6t + 3, t ⩾ 1, 是否存在 v 阶柯克曼三元系大集？
    22	
    23	**Translation:** “For every v=6t+3 with integer t≥1, does a large set of Kirkman triple systems of order v exist?” The source is book p.428 / PDF p.444, `sources/book-excerpt.txt`, lines24–25. This is a **universal existence question**. Constructing57 or87 would settle a new case, **not solve the book's question**.
    24	
    25	### (b) Dated primary attestation and current-status search
    26	
    27	**Current status: uncertified-open.** The latest located primary attestation is Chen Wang, [arXiv:2607.21521](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.21521), dated **2026-07-23**. After defining LKTS with the same resolvability and disjointness conditions, §1 says that the existence problem “remains far from settled”; §4 explicitly identifies57 and87 as the two possible exceptions below200 and smallest remaining orders. This attests the book-wide problem and the two cases as unresolved **in that source at that date**. It is not a formal certification that no later resolution exists.
    28	
    29	The **2026-10-14 search log is labelled “no resolution found; not proof of openness”**: [`sources/status-search-20261014.md`](sources/status-search-20261014.md). It includes arXiv/journal searches and works citing the key sources. OpenAlex returned zero indexed works citing Wang2026 and20 citing Chang–Zhou2013; the latter list includes Liu–Lei2023, Xu–Ji2024, Tan–Zhou2025, and a June2026 coding-theory article whose primary abstract was inspected. Index coverage and inaccessible bodies remain explicit limitations.
    30	
    31	Parent-reported **openai/math corpus screen**: no matching family was found in the local clone at `adc7f1241` or in the remote listing checked **2026-10-14**. That screen was not replayed by this worker and is not proof of open status.
    32	
    33	The book's smallest case21 was solved by Zhou–Chang, together with39, using multiplier automorphism groups: [DOI10.1007/s10623-009-9325-8](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10623-009-9325-8), online2009-09-16 / journal2010. Wang's broader below200 spectrum is a source statement, not independently replayed here; the original-body access caveat for Xu–Ji's family explaining147 remains in `sources/recursion-notes.md` §2.3.
    34	
    35	### (c) Fully specified statements attacked
    36	
    37	For each fixed **v∈{57,87}**, let `X={0,…,v−1}`. Seek families `P[i,j]⊆binom(X,3)`, with `0≤i≤v−3`, `0≤j≤(v−3)/2`, such that: (1) each `P[i,j]` partitionsX; (2) for eachi every pair ofX belongs to exactly one block of `⋃j P[i,j]`; and (3) every triple ofX belongs to exactly one `P[i,j]` over all i,j. Blocks are unordered three-element subsets, with no multiplicity. This is the unrestricted finite target, not the universal book assertion.
    38	
    39	The model restrictions are fixed separately: cyclic development on `Z_(v−2)` plus two individually fixed infinities; sampled rainbow infinity matchings followed by finite-triple exact cover and unrestricted resolution; `C_19` acting on three19-point orbits with prescribed constituent-orbit patterns; and, for57, regular development by `F_55=C_11⋊C_5` plus two fixed infinities. The nonabelian multiplication is `(x,y)(x',y')=(x+3^y x' mod11,y+y' mod5)`. A negative result in one model is not a negative result for the unrestricted target. The extended solver parameters and modeling choices were recorded before the runs in [`sources/extended-search-protocol.md`](sources/extended-search-protocol.md).
    40	
    41	The initial portfolio produced verified9/15 certificates and restricted proofs but no target certificate. The explicitly requested multi-hour continuation is being recorded separately; no running search is counted as a completed result.
