   122	
   123	### Stronger mathematical conclusions from the failed symmetry route
   124	
   125	The complete written arguments are in [`proof.md`](proof.md). In particular:
   126	
   127	1. **Fixed-point divisibility.** If an involution preserves a KTS(v) and its chosen resolution, and has f fixed points and c fixed parallel classes, then `2cf=f²−2f+v`; hence **f divides v**.
   128	2. **All nonidentity scalar multipliers are excluded** in the cyclic base models at both targets, when both infinities are individually fixed and the chosen base resolution is invariant. The involutions modulo 55 have f=7,13,3; modulo 85 they have f=7,19,3. Divisibility excludes the first two; infinity-fixing negation is excluded by repeated finite pairs. Even-order multipliers reduce to these; the remaining order-5 case modulo 55 would partition seven fixed points into triples.
   129	3. Negation **swapping** infinities at 87 is impossible already for the STS: the one-fixed-point involution gives the odd parity obstruction `binom(43,2)=903`. This does not exclude the corresponding order-57 model.
   130	4. For the alternative action of `C_19` on three 19-point orbits at 57, an invariant **resolved** large set must have **17 or 36** fixed constituents, never 55. Its other constituents form one or two free 19-orbits. No search of those mixed-constituent cases was completed here.
   131	
   132	Item 2 is a mathematical exclusion stronger than the two UNKNOWN multiplier runs; their logged statuses remain UNKNOWN. It does **not** exclude arbitrary cyclic LKTSs, multipliers preserving blocks without an invariant resolution, or noncyclic constructions. No novelty is claimed for these lemmas. `work/screen_symmetries.py` checks all **104 units** of the two rings and records the applicable obstruction; only identity survives.
   133	
   134	### Recursive attacks actually attempted
   135	
   136	Detailed definitions, counts, proofs, and missing compatibility conditions are in [`sources/recursion-notes.md`](sources/recursion-notes.md), §§3–5.
   137	
   138	- **57=4·15−3:** a frame of type `14^4` filled with four KTS(15)s sharing one point gives a single KTS(57), but keeping those four holes fixed permits at most **13** disjoint systems and omits all `∞AB` and `AAB` triples. A precise sufficient replacement is **13 compatible frames of type `14^4` plus 42 compatible frames of type `2^28`**, using an explicit cross-group 1-factorization. Each latter frame needs **364 AAB and 140 ABC** triples. The simultaneous global triple partition, not individual-frame existence, is unproved.
   139	- **87=4·21+3:** an ordinary frame of type `21^4` is impossible (21/2 missing-group classes); an STS(24,3) is impossible (outside-point replication 23/2); synchronizing four KTS(21) resolutions repeats the stem triple. An exact alternative deletes a three-point stem and asks for **three coupled matching arrays over 85 colors**, followed by compatible triangle decompositions/resolutions of the residual graphs on 84 points. One color needs 1120 pure-outside triangles, the other 84 need 1121 each. The matching compatibility and globally disjoint triangle resolutions remain unconstructed.
   140	
   141	These are genuine failed routes and precise unresolved auxiliary objects, not new unconditional `4v±3` recursions.
