     1	# C19-invariant resolved LKTS(57): obstruction and staged search
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-10-14 (PT). This note and `work/c19_search.py` concern the specified action on `Z_19 × {0,1,2}`, not arbitrary large sets. A point `(x,i)` is encoded as `19i+x`. Fixed constituents are required to have an invariant chosen resolution.
     4	
     5	## 1. The 36-fixed pattern is impossible
     6	
     7	A fixed KTS(57) has 28 classes. Under C19 their orbits have lengths 1 or 19, so there are either 9 or 28 fixed classes. A fixed class consists of one orbit of 19 triples. Each triple must meet all three layers, since its orbit must partition the 57 points. Thus 28 fixed classes would contain only ABC triples and could not cover within-layer pairs. There are exactly nine fixed classes and one free orbit of 19 classes.
     8	
     9	Choose one class P from that free class orbit. All its 19 triples belong to distinct triple-orbits: two translated blocks in P would make the translated classes overlap, contrary to a resolution. Along with the nine fixed-class seed orbits, these are 28 distinct triple-orbits. Write u,w,z for the numbers of AAA, AAB, ABC blocks in P. All 27 within-layer pair-orbits must occur exactly once in P, so
    10	
    11	`3u+w=27`, `u+w+z=19`, and hence `z=2u−8`, `u≥4`.
    12	
    13	There are 51 AAA triple-orbits per layer, but **nine per layer cannot occur in any C19-fixed STS**, regardless of resolution. They are the arithmetic progression orbits represented by `{−d,0,d}`, with `d=1,…,9`. In such a block the two consecutive pairs belong to the same translation orbit; developing the block repeats pairs. Conversely, a three-element subset of Z19 has two pairs in the same unsigned difference orbit exactly when it is an arithmetic progression: after choosing their common endpoint, the other endpoints have opposite nonzero differences. The AP center is unique because 3 is invertible modulo 19, so these are exactly nine distinct orbits.
    14	
    15	Consequently, only `3·(51−9)=126` AAA orbits are available to fixed STSs. Disjoint fixed resolved constituents each need at least four. In particular,
    16	
    17	`36·4=144>126`.
    18	
    19	This is a rigorous obstruction to the **entire 36-fixed + one free 19-orbit pattern**, not merely the staged search below. Together with the constituent orbit counts and the existing exclusion of 55 fixed constituents, only **17 fixed constituents plus two free 19-orbits** remain for this resolved C19 action. The bound does not exclude an automorphism of unresolved block systems whose chosen resolutions are not invariant.
    20	
    21	The implementation exhaustively inventories all 1,540 triple-orbits and 84 pair-orbits, checks their lengths, identifies precisely the 27 AP orbits, and independently expands every triple-orbit to check whether its 57 pair incidences are distinct. The proof above does not rely on a negative solver answer.
    22	
    23	## 2. Exact fixed-constituent model
    24	
    25	For each admissible, non-forbidden triple-orbit and each of its 19 translates, a Boolean variable means that the translated block belongs to P. For each non-forbidden ABC orbit there is a second variable meaning that the full orbit is a fixed class. Exact-one constraints cover each of the 57 points of P and each of the 84 pair-orbits. They force 19 P blocks and nine fixed classes, and prevent repeated triple-orbits automatically. The generated constituent is expanded into 28 classes and all 1,596 concrete pairs are checked independently of the solver.
    26	
    27	`--fixed-aaa` freezes u (default 4). Thus the default starter has four AAA, fifteen AAB and no ABC blocks. This is a search restriction, not a theorem that every possible constituent has this type.
    28	
    29	Seventeen constituents are generated greedily, forbidding all previously used orbits. A failed candidate solve discards that greedy prefix and restarts with a new recorded seed. Each prefix, including abandoned ones, has its own saved packing file and solver receipts. The first complete packing is frozen. This does not enumerate all possible collections of 17 constituents.
    30	
    31	## 3. Exact free-base completion of a frozen packing
    32	
    33	Seventeen fixed constituents use 476 triple-orbits. The complement has 1,064 orbits. For each remaining orbit, each of its 19 blocks, each of two free bases, and each permitted class, a Boolean variable places that block in the base/class. Constraints require:
    34	
    35	- exactly one placement overall for every remaining triple-orbit;
    36	- exactly one occurrence of each concrete pair in each base;
    37	- exactly one occurrence of each point in every class of each base.
    38	
    39	Thus both bases are resolved KTS(57)s, each using 532 distinct triple-orbits. Their 38 translates and the 17 frozen constituents partition all triples. No additional automorphism of a free base is required.
    40	
    41	Class-permutation symmetry is removed losslessly by sorting classes by the least other point in the block through point 0. In class c (zero-based), this least point lies between c+1 and 2c+1: the blocks through 0 form a perfect matching on points 1,…,56. The two bases are ordered by the third point of their block through pair {0,1}; those third points differ because their blocks cannot share an orbit. This removes only interchange of the two bases.
    42	
