    23	                                              A Steiner triple system of order v, denoted by STS(v), is a pair (X, B), where X
    24	                                           is a v-set and B is a collection of triples of X, called blocks, such that every pair of
    25	                                           points of X is contained in a unique block of B. Let (X, B) be an STS(v). If there
    26	                                           exists a partition
    27	                                                                          A = {P1 , P2 , . . . , P(v−1)/2 }
    28	                                           of B such that every Pi is a parallel class, that is, a partition of X, then the STS(v)
    29	                                           is called resolvable, and A is called a resolution. A resolvable STS(v) is usually
    30	                                           called a Kirkman triple system of order v, briefly a KTS(v). It is well known that
    31	                                           a KTS(v) exists if and only if v ≡ 3 (mod 6).
    32	                                              Two STS(v)s on the same point set are said to be disjoint if they have no blocks
    33	                                           in common. By a simple counting argument, there can be at most v − 2 mutually
    34	                                           disjoint STS(v)s on the same point set. A set of v − 2 mutually disjoint STS(v)s
    35	                                           necessarily partitions all triples of the point set and is called a large set of Steiner
    36	                                           triple systems, denoted by LSTS(v). A large set of Kirkman triple systems of
    37	                                           order v, denoted by LKTS(v), is an LSTS(v) in which every constituent STS(v) is
    38	                                           resolvable.
